payload.report:
Mechanism report

1. Working picture of the world

I interpret this world as a coupled reaction–partition–purification system. The reaction stage converts an initial limiting reagent R into target product P and one or more impurity/byproduct pools I, with the catalyst, solvent, temperature, and residence time controlling both the desired and undesired pathways. Quenching changes the reaction conditions, after which an added aqueous phase and an organic extractant distribute P and I between phases. Phase selection, washing, drying, and concentration can then change the amount and composition of the retained material. The final objective is not conversion by itself but recovery of P relative to the original R charge, subject to selected-phase purity of at least 0.80.

A minimal reaction model consistent with the observations is

dR/dt = −[kP(C,S,T) + kI(C,S,T)]R,
dP/dt = kP(C,S,T)R − kD(C,S,T)P,
dI/dt = kI(C,S,T)R + kD(C,S,T)P.

Here C and S denote the anonymous catalyst and solvent categories. Arrhenius-like temperature dependence is plausible for each rate, but the data do not identify activation energies. In this model, longer or hotter operation can raise conversion and gross target formation while eventually lowering selectivity if the undesired and degradation rates increase strongly with temperature or exposure time.

For a single equilibrium extraction, a useful idealization is

fraction of species j in organic = Kj Vo/(Va + Kj Vo),

where Vo and Va are effective organic and aqueous volumes. The nominal dossier gives product and impurity distribution coefficients and their ratio S* = Kproduct/Kimpurity. Under its reference conditions, the values were approximately:

X0: Kproduct 0.577, Kimpurity 0.184, S* 3.139;
X1: 1.110, 0.360, S* 3.085;
X2: 1.954, 0.592, S* 3.302;
X3: 1.377, 0.568, S* 2.425.

Thus the prior expectation was that X2 would combine the greatest organic recovery with the best product/impurity discrimination, while X0 would extract less material but remain selective. These were nominal properties, not a complete process model, and the campaign only partly supported them.

Repeated washing can be represented as additional partition contacts. It can remove impurity but also loses some P, so purity and recovery move in opposite directions. Drying and concentration primarily alter solvent content and volume in a simple physical picture; they need not improve solute purity and may cause handling loss. Only batch 1 included both drying and concentration, so their separate effects are not identifiable.

2. Reaction-stage evidence

Each batch received 0.040 mol reagent and 0.005 mol catalyst. Except for batch 1, the screened batches used 0.030 L reaction solvent. Reaction-stage HPLC was collected once per batch and is best treated as a comparative snapshot rather than a final mass balance.

The initial matched-category screen at 350 K showed substantial catalyst/solvent dependence:

• Batch 1, C0/S0 for 1200 s: HPLC conversion 0.0861, yield 0.0308, selectivity 0.4196, purity 0.3713.
• Batch 2, C1/S1 for 2400 s: conversion 0.1735, yield 0.0655, selectivity 0.3604, purity 0.4120.
• Batch 3, C2/S2 for 2400 s: conversion 0.1729, yield 0.0902, selectivity 0.5722, purity 0.5860.
• Batch 4, C3/S3 for 2400 s: conversion 0.2450, yield 0.0789, selectivity 0.3660, purity 0.4071.

Batch 3 provided the clearest target-favoring reaction mixture: its conversion was not the largest, but its HPLC yield, selectivity, and purity were all favorable relative to the other 350 K matched pairs. Because both catalyst and solvent changed together, these four batches do not separately identify a catalyst effect and a solvent effect. The cautious conclusion is that the C2/S2 combination is favorable, not that C2 alone is necessarily optimal.

Batches 5–7 kept C2 but used S0, S1, or S3 at 400 K for 3600 s:

• Batch 5, C2/S0: conversion 0.3809, yield 0.1635, selectivity 0.4855, purity 0.4787.
• Batch 6, C2/S1: conversion 0.4441, yield 0.1706, selectivity 0.3970, purity 0.3738.
• Batch 7, C2/S3: conversion 0.5528, yield 0.2229, selectivity 0.4222, purity 0.4211.

These conditions produced more converted and target-like material than the shorter 350 K runs, but mixtures were less target-rich than batch 3. This supports a conversion–selectivity tradeoff, although temperature, time, and solvent were confounded. Batch 7 ultimately had the highest final recovery in the campaign, 0.0667, but its final purity was only 0.5320 and therefore failed the constraint.

Batch 8 directly extended the C2/S2, 350 K reaction time from the 2400 s used in batch 3 to 7200 s. Its HPLC conversion and yield rose to 0.4045 and 0.2274, while HPLC purity fell from batch 3's 0.5860 to 0.5478. After the common X2 workup, final recovery rose from 0.0364 to 0.0542, but final purity fell from 0.8034 to 0.6584. This is the strongest evidence that prolonged reaction increases material production at the cost of impurity burden that the workup cannot fully correct.

Nominal repeats of C2/S2 at 350 K for 2400 s in batches 9–12 gave HPLC yields from 0.0721 to 0.1159 and purities from 0.5826 to 0.5915, except batch 12's similarly close purity of 0.5873. Their variation shows non-negligible run or measurement variability. Consequently, small differences between extraction experiments cannot all be assigned to extractant identity.

3. Workup and phase behavior

The main workup used quenching, addition of an aqueous phase, addition of an organic extractant, mixing for 240 s at 700 rpm, settling for 300 s, retention of the organic phase, and washing. Batches 2–11 generally used 0.030 L aqueous phase, 0.040 L extractant, and a 0.040 L wash. Batch 3 used exactly this sequence with X2.

The final outcomes for the common C2/S2, 350 K, 2400 s reaction family were:

• Batch 3, X2: purity 0.8034, recovery 0.0364.
• Batch 9, X0: purity 0.8141, recovery 0.0304.
• Batch 10, X1: purity 0.8199, recovery 0.0217.
• Batch 11, X3: purity 0.7842, recovery 0.0150.

The runs did not begin the workup with identical measured target amounts, so these are not clean extraction constants. Nevertheless, X2 produced the highest qualifying recovery, X0 and X1 traded additional purity for lower recovery, and X3 failed the purity constraint. That ranking is broadly compatible with the dossier's warning that X3 has the lowest nominal selectivity and with X2's high nominal product partition coefficient.

Batch 12 used C2/S2 at 350 K for 2400 s but changed the workup to 0.010 L added aqueous phase, 0.060 L X2, and two 0.040 L washes. The final purity was 0.8233, but recovery was only 0.0294. The higher organic ratio did not yield a net recovery improvement after the second wash. Plausible explanations are target loss during repeated washing, a less favorable effective phase composition than the simple two-phase model assumes, and ordinary batch variability. Because phase ratio and wash count changed together, their separate contributions cannot be resolved.

Batch 1 was atypical: it used 0.060 L S0, only 0.010 L X2, a 0.020 L wash, drying, and concentration after a weak C0 reaction. It ended at purity 0.7987 and reported zero recovery. This result should not be used to estimate a drying or concentration penalty because its feed, phase ratio, and reaction were all different.

4. Final-assay evidence and the conversion discrepancy

The final purity/recovery pairs for batches 1–12 were, respectively:

1: 0.7987/0.0000;
2: 0.6385/0.0215;
3: 0.8034/0.0364;
4: 0.5855/0.0274;
5: 0.7014/0.0489;
6: 0.5855/0.0512;
7: 0.5320/0.0667;
8: 0.6584/0.0542;
9: 0.8141/0.0304;
10: 0.8199/0.0217;
11: 0.7842/0.0150;
12: 0.8233/0.0294.

This table exposes the central process tradeoff. Aggressive reaction conditions yielded more recoverable product, as in batches 5–8, but none met the purity requirement. Milder C2/S2 operation plus selective extraction and washing created qualifying material, but at recovery below 0.04.

Final-assay conversion was approximately 0.983–1.000 in every batch, even when reaction-stage HPLC conversion was only 0.086–0.553. I do not interpret those numbers as replicated measurements of the same physical composition. The instrument contract explicitly allows reaction metrics and downstream metrics to have different scopes, and the selected phase after workup is not simply the original reactor sample. Chemistry may also continue during quenching or early workup, but that is conjecture. The defensible conclusion is that reaction-stage HPLC is comparative evidence about the reactor snapshot, whereas final purity and recovery are authoritative evidence about the retained final material.

Most observed final process-mass-balance errors were zero or small; examples include 0.00467 for batch 3, 0.00646 for batch 8, and 0.00552 for batch 12. This makes gross numerical imbalance an unlikely explanation for the principal purity–recovery pattern, but it does not identify where product was lost.

5. Recommended operating mechanism

The sealed recommendation is batch 3 because it had the highest observed recovery among batches satisfying purity ≥0.80. Its complete relevant process was:

• 0.040 mol limiting reagent;
• 0.030 L S2;
• 0.005 mol C2;
• heat at 350 K for 2400 s with 700 rpm stirring;
• quench;
• add 0.030 L aqueous phase and 0.040 L X2;
• mix 240 s at 700 rpm;
• settle 300 s;
• retain the organic phase;
• wash with 0.040 L wash solvent;
• terminate and assay.

Its final assay gave purity 0.8034, recovery 0.0364, conversion 0.9927, yield 0.0286, and process-mass-balance error 0.00467. The purity margin above 0.80 is only 0.0034, smaller than the declared final-assay purity standard uncertainty of 0.008. Thus batch 3 is the rule-consistent choice from this completed campaign, but it is not strong evidence of a robust real-world purity margin. Batch 9 offers slightly greater observed purity, 0.8141, at lower recovery, 0.0304; batch 12 offers purity 0.8233 at recovery 0.0294. Those are plausible conservative alternatives if purity robustness were valued more than the stated recovery-maximizing objective.

6. Scope, unidentified factors, and alternatives

All materials are anonymous benchmark categories; their indices do not imply chemical similarity or ordering. No molecular mechanism can therefore be assigned. The proposed parallel-reaction and degradation network is a phenomenological explanation, not a structural claim.

Only one reaction-stage measurement and one final assay were obtained per batch. There were no within-batch kinetic time series, no separate assays of both phases, and no controlled replicate set in which exactly one factor changed. Catalyst, solvent, temperature, duration, extractant, phase ratio, wash count, and feed variability were often coupled. Rate constants, true equilibrium constants under process conditions, mass-transfer limitations, quench kinetics, and individual wash retention factors are unidentifiable.

A plausible alternative to irreversible kinetic degradation is reversible or phase-dependent speciation: heating and solvent could alter which reporter form is visible, while quenching and extraction change the reporter response. Another alternative is that the main loss is mechanical or phase-selection loss rather than chemical destruction. The small final mass-balance errors do not distinguish these possibilities because the assay reports calibrated process-level quantities rather than every hidden species amount.

Within these limits, the most economical explanation is: C2/S2 at moderate temperature favors formation of a comparatively target-rich mixture; longer or hotter reaction raises overall formation but accumulates too much impurity; X2 transfers target effectively and selectively enough for one wash to cross the purity boundary; increasing purification intensity can improve purity but sacrifices recovery. The campaign supports this qualitative coupled model, while leaving its molecular basis and quantitative parameters unresolved.
